1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Here are two squares, each told
to rotate by a quarter turn.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:05,660
They both end up in the same place.

3
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:08,020
But watch the blue one again.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:10,380
Halfway through, it shrinks.

5
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:12,740
Now let's ask both for half a turn.

6
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:14,700
The yellow square spins around.

7
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:16,820
The blue one does something much stranger:

8
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:20,760
it collapses to a single point
and turns itself inside out.

9
00:00:20,760 --> 00:00:23,640
Same ending, completely different journey.

10
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:26,660
By the end of this episode,
you will know exactly why,

11
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:32,550
and the explanation reveals how every
animation in Manim actually works.

12
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:35,590
Every animation in Manim, from
a simple fade to a full morph,

13
00:00:35,590 --> 00:00:37,410
is built on one idea.

14
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:39,610
You have a starting
state, an ending state,

15
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:42,610
and a number called alpha
that runs from zero to one.

16
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:44,710
When alpha is zero, you see the start.

17
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:46,550
When alpha is one, you see the end.

18
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:50,510
And for any alpha in between, Manim
computes the state that far along the way.

19
00:00:50,510 --> 00:00:53,050
For a single point, "that
far along" is simple.

20
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:56,490
Take the start, and move a fraction
alpha of the way toward the end.

21
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:59,070
At one half, you are
exactly in the middle.

22
00:00:59,070 --> 00:01:01,470
Mathematicians call this
linear interpolation,

23
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:03,770
and you will often hear
it shortened to lerp.

24
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:06,970
A shape is just many points, so
to morph a square into a circle,

25
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:10,670
Manim pairs every point on the
square with a point on the circle,

26
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:15,150
and moves each one a fraction alpha
of the way along its own little path.

27
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:16,610
All of them, at the same time,

28
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:18,050
driven by the same alpha.

29
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:19,510
And where does alpha come from?

30
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:23,390
From the clock. A one-second animation
sampled fifteen times a second,

31
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:25,310
the rate ManimOnline uses for previews,

32
00:01:25,310 --> 00:01:28,570
takes fifteen snapshots, each
with alpha a little larger.

33
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:29,950
That is the whole engine.

34
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:32,183
Starts, ends, and alpha.

35
00:01:32,183 --> 00:01:33,763
There is a small twist.

36
00:01:33,763 --> 00:01:35,743
Alpha does not have to grow steadily.

37
00:01:35,743 --> 00:01:38,143
Between the clock and
alpha sits a rate function,

38
00:01:38,143 --> 00:01:39,483
which can bend time.

39
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:43,183
If alpha simply equals time,
that is the linear rate function.

40
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:45,083
Steady speed from start to finish.

41
00:01:45,083 --> 00:01:47,123
It looks mechanical, like a conveyor belt,

42
00:01:47,123 --> 00:01:50,563
because nothing in the real
world starts and stops instantly.

43
00:01:50,563 --> 00:01:52,943
Manim's default is called smooth.

44
00:01:52,943 --> 00:01:54,963
Its graph is an S-shaped curve:

45
00:01:54,963 --> 00:01:57,263
flat at the start, steep in the middle,

46
00:01:57,263 --> 00:01:58,623
flat again at the end.

47
00:01:58,623 --> 00:02:01,203
So objects ease out of rest, cruise,

48
00:02:01,203 --> 00:02:02,563
and ease into place.

49
00:02:02,563 --> 00:02:07,843
That gentle acceleration is a large
part of why Manim animations feel calm.

50
00:02:07,843 --> 00:02:09,103
There are many more.

51
00:02:09,103 --> 00:02:12,243
Rush into starts slowly and finishes fast.

52
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:13,843
Rush from does the opposite.

53
00:02:13,843 --> 00:02:16,683
And there and back is
not even a one-way trip:

54
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:18,503
alpha climbs to one at the halfway mark,

55
00:02:18,503 --> 00:02:22,323
and then returns to zero,
so the motion undoes itself.

56
00:02:22,323 --> 00:02:25,803
This code races five dots,
one per rate function,

57
00:02:25,803 --> 00:02:27,823
and they all start and finish together,

58
00:02:27,823 --> 00:02:30,183
except the one that comes home.

59
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:34,263
You choose a rate function per
animation by passing rate func.

60
00:02:34,263 --> 00:02:35,483
Try adding your own:

61
00:02:35,483 --> 00:02:39,603
any Python function that takes a number
from zero to one and returns a number

62
00:02:39,603 --> 00:02:41,183
will do.

63
00:02:41,183 --> 00:02:43,943
Manim comes with dozens
of ready-made animations,

64
00:02:43,943 --> 00:02:46,103
and they fall into a few families.

65
00:02:46,103 --> 00:02:47,343
Some bring objects in.

66
00:02:47,343 --> 00:02:48,723
Create traces an outline.

67
00:02:48,723 --> 00:02:53,083
Write is designed for text, drawing
strokes and then filling them.

68
00:02:53,083 --> 00:02:56,283
Grow from center expands
an object from nothing.

69
00:02:56,283 --> 00:02:57,703
Fade in materialises it,

70
00:02:57,703 --> 00:03:01,103
and you can pass a shift so
it drifts in from a direction.

71
00:03:01,103 --> 00:03:02,423
Some draw attention.

72
00:03:02,423 --> 00:03:05,383
Indicate briefly enlarges
and recolours an object,

73
00:03:05,383 --> 00:03:07,123
then returns it to normal.

74
00:03:07,123 --> 00:03:09,583
Circumscribe traces a shape around it,

75
00:03:09,583 --> 00:03:12,063
like a teacher circling
something on the board.

76
00:03:12,063 --> 00:03:13,623
Some change an object:

77
00:03:13,623 --> 00:03:16,263
Rotate, Transform, and many more.

78
00:03:16,263 --> 00:03:17,843
And some take objects away.

79
00:03:17,843 --> 00:03:22,063
Fade out and Uncreate remove the
object from the scene when they finish,

80
00:03:22,063 --> 00:03:23,583
so you do not have to.

81
00:03:23,583 --> 00:03:25,203
Not every exit works that way,

82
00:03:25,203 --> 00:03:29,043
though: Shrink to center leaves
a zero-sized object behind,

83
00:03:29,043 --> 00:03:31,863
still in the scene, invisible but present.

84
00:03:31,863 --> 00:03:34,883
Scan this one and read the
code alongside the animation.

85
00:03:34,883 --> 00:03:36,223
It is almost a dictionary:

86
00:03:36,223 --> 00:03:38,983
one line, one effect.

87
00:03:38,983 --> 00:03:41,803
You have already met the most
flexible animation of all:

88
00:03:41,803 --> 00:03:44,963
the word animate. Write
square dot animate,

89
00:03:44,963 --> 00:03:46,863
followed by any chain of methods,

90
00:03:46,863 --> 00:03:48,483
and Manim does something clever.

91
00:03:48,483 --> 00:03:50,143
It makes a copy of the square,

92
00:03:50,143 --> 00:03:52,603
applies those methods
to the copy instantly,

93
00:03:52,603 --> 00:03:54,623
and uses the result as the target.

94
00:03:54,623 --> 00:03:58,543
Then it builds an animation from
the current square to that target.

95
00:03:58,543 --> 00:04:02,583
Any method that changes a mobject
can become an animation this way,

96
00:04:02,583 --> 00:04:04,883
which is why animate is everywhere.

97
00:04:04,883 --> 00:04:06,643
Now we can solve the mystery.

98
00:04:06,643 --> 00:04:08,563
The blue square was rotated with animate.

99
00:04:08,563 --> 00:04:10,743
So Manim worked out the target,

100
00:04:10,743 --> 00:04:12,443
the square turned a quarter turn,

101
00:04:12,443 --> 00:04:14,563
and then did what every animation does:

102
00:04:14,563 --> 00:04:19,543
it moved each point in a straight line
from where it starts to where it ends.

103
00:04:19,543 --> 00:04:20,523
Follow one corner.

104
00:04:20,523 --> 00:04:22,523
It does not travel around a circle.

105
00:04:22,523 --> 00:04:25,263
It cuts straight across, like a shortcut.

106
00:04:25,263 --> 00:04:27,183
Do that with all four corners,

107
00:04:27,183 --> 00:04:30,263
and halfway through they
form a smaller square,

108
00:04:30,263 --> 00:04:32,643
about seventy one percent of the size.

109
00:04:32,643 --> 00:04:34,983
For a half turn it gets more dramatic.

110
00:04:34,983 --> 00:04:37,623
Every corner's destination
is the opposite corner,

111
00:04:37,623 --> 00:04:41,783
and the straight line between opposite
corners goes right through the centre.

112
00:04:41,783 --> 00:04:45,223
So at the halfway point, all
four corners meet in the middle,

113
00:04:45,223 --> 00:04:47,423
and the square vanishes for an instant.

114
00:04:47,423 --> 00:04:49,403
The yellow square used Rotate,

115
00:04:49,403 --> 00:04:52,083
an animation that knows it is a rotation.

116
00:04:52,083 --> 00:04:55,003
It doesn't interpolate
between a start and an end;

117
00:04:55,003 --> 00:04:59,103
at every moment it turns the
original by a fraction of the angle.

118
00:04:59,103 --> 00:05:00,823
Every corner travels on a circle,

119
00:05:00,823 --> 00:05:02,803
and the square stays rigid.

120
00:05:02,803 --> 00:05:06,163
So the rule is: animate
is perfect for moving,

121
00:05:06,163 --> 00:05:07,883
scaling and recolouring.

122
00:05:07,883 --> 00:05:11,383
When you mean "spin", reach for Rotate.

123
00:05:11,383 --> 00:05:14,523
Give play several animations,
and they run at the same time.

124
00:05:14,523 --> 00:05:15,963
That covers most needs.

125
00:05:15,963 --> 00:05:18,043
But sometimes you want
more control over timing,

126
00:05:18,043 --> 00:05:20,143
and there are three tools for that.

127
00:05:20,143 --> 00:05:23,603
Animation group bundles animations
together so they can be treated as one,

128
00:05:23,603 --> 00:05:25,383
and by default they all start together.

129
00:05:25,383 --> 00:05:29,163
Lagged start begins each animation
a little after the previous one.

130
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:31,643
How much later is controlled by lag ratio.

131
00:05:31,643 --> 00:05:35,503
A lag ratio of zero point five means
each one starts when the previous one is

132
00:05:35,503 --> 00:05:37,363
halfway done, so they overlap,

133
00:05:37,363 --> 00:05:38,643
like a ripple.

134
00:05:38,643 --> 00:05:40,603
Succession plays them
strictly one after another.

135
00:05:40,603 --> 00:05:43,283
Each waits for the previous one to finish.

136
00:05:43,283 --> 00:05:47,163
Lagged starts are behind a lot of the
most satisfying motion in math animations.

137
00:05:47,163 --> 00:05:48,163
In this second example,

138
00:05:48,163 --> 00:05:52,283
sixteen bars each stretch up and back
down with the there-and-back rate

139
00:05:52,283 --> 00:05:57,533
function, and a small lag turns
sixteen identical motions into a wave.

140
00:05:57,533 --> 00:06:02,353
In episode one, I said that Transform
makes the square circle-shaped,

141
00:06:02,353 --> 00:06:05,053
and that the circle is
only used as a target.

142
00:06:05,053 --> 00:06:06,353
Here is why that matters.

143
00:06:06,353 --> 00:06:10,153
After Transform a into b, the
thing on screen looks like b,

144
00:06:10,153 --> 00:06:12,433
but it is still the object named a.

145
00:06:12,433 --> 00:06:15,793
The object named b was
never added to the scene.

146
00:06:15,793 --> 00:06:18,133
So if you later want to move what you see,

147
00:06:18,133 --> 00:06:19,593
you must animate a.

148
00:06:19,593 --> 00:06:23,053
That can feel backwards, so Manim
offers Replacement transform.

149
00:06:23,053 --> 00:06:26,273
After Replacement transform c into d,

150
00:06:26,273 --> 00:06:28,653
the object c is removed from the scene,

151
00:06:28,653 --> 00:06:30,393
and d takes its place.

152
00:06:30,393 --> 00:06:32,933
The circle on screen really is d.

153
00:06:32,933 --> 00:06:34,533
Neither is right or wrong.

154
00:06:34,533 --> 00:06:38,453
Use Transform when you want to keep
talking about the same object as it changes

155
00:06:38,453 --> 00:06:42,753
shape. Use Replacement transform when
you would rather keep using the name of

156
00:06:42,753 --> 00:06:46,473
the new thing. The trouble only starts
when you forget which one you used,

157
00:06:46,473 --> 00:06:49,783
and animate an object
that is not on screen.

158
00:06:49,783 --> 00:06:51,863
Knowing how to animate is one thing.

159
00:06:51,863 --> 00:06:54,683
Knowing how long an animation
should take is a craft,

160
00:06:54,683 --> 00:06:56,063
and it's worth a minute.

161
00:06:56,063 --> 00:06:58,823
The default of one second
is a good starting point,

162
00:06:58,823 --> 00:07:02,683
but a viewer needs more time for
a big change than a small one.

163
00:07:02,683 --> 00:07:05,923
A dot sliding a short
distance can be quick.

164
00:07:05,923 --> 00:07:08,423
A whole equation rearranging itself,

165
00:07:08,423 --> 00:07:10,563
or a grid bending into a new shape,

166
00:07:10,563 --> 00:07:12,203
deserves two or three seconds,

167
00:07:12,203 --> 00:07:15,343
because the viewer is trying to
follow several things at once.

168
00:07:15,343 --> 00:07:18,283
New text needs time to be read,

169
00:07:18,283 --> 00:07:20,343
and the time grows with its length.

170
00:07:20,343 --> 00:07:23,223
If something appears and
vanishes before it can be read,

171
00:07:23,223 --> 00:07:24,943
it might as well not have appeared.

172
00:07:24,943 --> 00:07:27,723
And pauses are not wasted time.

173
00:07:27,723 --> 00:07:32,443
A short wait after an important reveal
gives the viewer a moment to think,

174
00:07:32,443 --> 00:07:37,303
"oh, I see". The most common mistake
in first animations isn't anything

175
00:07:37,303 --> 00:07:40,203
technical. It's simply going too fast.

176
00:07:40,203 --> 00:07:43,143
If you plan to add narration,
write the words first,

177
00:07:43,143 --> 00:07:45,703
and time the animations to them.

178
00:07:45,703 --> 00:07:47,423
That's how this series is made:

179
00:07:47,423 --> 00:07:50,800
every play and wait is
matched to a sentence.

180
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:52,940
Every animation is a start, an end,

181
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:55,640
and alpha running from zero to one,

182
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:57,620
bent by a rate function.

183
00:07:57,620 --> 00:07:59,740
Animate turns any change
into an animation,

184
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:03,640
by moving every point in a
straight line toward the target.

185
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:05,360
That is perfect for most changes,

186
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:07,560
and exactly wrong for rotations,

187
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:09,380
which is what Rotate is for.

188
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:14,000
And groups, lagged starts and
successions arrange animations in time.

189
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,740
But there was a phrase
we used casually today:

190
00:08:16,740 --> 00:08:18,120
"every point on the shape".

191
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:20,060
What points? How many?

192
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:22,220
And how does a square, with four corners,

193
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:25,460
morph into a circle, which has none?

194
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:26,700
That is next time,

195
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:30,435
and it is one of my favourite
ideas in all of computer graphics.
